            <ArticleTitle>A synthesis of selected national Australian guidelines on the general practice management of adult patients who are overweight or obese.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>327-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A general practitioner's (GP's) daily workload includes an ever-increasing number of patients who are overweight and obese. A number of Australian guidelines are aimed at assisting GPs who are managing this group of adult patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objective of this article is to synthesise selected national Australian guidelines for GPs in the management of adults who are overweight or obese.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Relevant national guidelines were chosen and reviewed by three GPs. The recommendations were amalgamated and any discrepancies were discussed within the research team.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We presented a synthesis of the guidelines under headings that will assist GPs in a structured approach to managing patients who are overweight and obese.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This synthesis forms the basis for developing a practical toolkit to assist GPs in managing adult patients who are overweight and obese.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
